{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 5766 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "187d00559b282c105880dcf917ea8485e335c45095944917ca1f2311d0dafb54" :edb/db-before-t 5765 :edb/db-after-t 5766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539078 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:58:26.040Z" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054345 1071 "![](images/b5/b531dcf3da55d8788a53411021a529cf76d5dfa0755612cd136d35d159aca4a3.png)\n\nUsing the graph above, find an approximate solution of the system\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = - 5 \\\\ 5 x - y = - 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054345 1071 "![](images/b5/b531dcf3da55d8788a53411021a529cf76d5dfa0755612cd136d35d159aca4a3.png)\n\nUsing the graph above, find an approximate solution of the system\n\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = - 5 \\\\ 5 x - y = - 3\\end{array} \\right. }$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054346 1071 "Fill in the table below, and use this to find a value of $x$ that corresponds to an approximate solution of the system\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 4 x + 2 \\\\ y = 2 x - 5.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ |  |  | $-3.6$ |  |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ |  |  | $-2.2$ |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ |  |  | $1.4$ |  |" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054346 1071 "Fill in the table below, and use this to find a value of $x$ that corresponds to an approximate solution of the system\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 4 x + 2 \\\\ y = 2 x - 5.\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ |  |  | $-3.6$ |  |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ |  |  | $-2.2$ |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ |  |  | $1.4$ |  |" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054347 1074 "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.2) + 6; = 11.676; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.2) + 3; = 11.58\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & |= & 11.676 - 11.58\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid 0.096 \\mid; = 0.096\n$$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.6) + 6; = 11.848; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.6) + 3; = 11.84\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & |= & 11.848 - 11.84\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid 0.008 \\mid; = 0.008\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054347 1074 "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.2) + 6; = 11.676; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.2) + 3; = 11.58$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & |= & 11.676 - 11.58\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid 0.096 \\mid; = 0.096$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.6) + 6; = 11.848; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.6) + 3; = 11.84$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} - y_{2} & |= & 11.848 - 11.84\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid 0.008 \\mid; = 0.008$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054369 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} S_i,\n$$ given that $I = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\},$ and $S_i = (i-1, \\,i+1).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054369 1071 "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} S_i$, given that $I = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\},$ and $S_i = (i-1, \\,i+1).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054370 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i\n$$  given that $I = \\mathbb{N}$ and $X_i = \\{ i, \\, 2i \\}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054370 1071 "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$  given that $I = \\mathbb{N}$ and $X_i = \\{ i, \\, 2i \\}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054420 1074 "First, we isolate the fractional term $\\dfrac{3x}{4}$ by adding $5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 &=-8\\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5+5 &=-8+5\\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}+0 &=-3 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4} &=-3\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ so we multiply by its reciprocal $$\n\\displaystyle \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x &=-3 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{4}x &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot (-3) \\\\[5pt]\n1 x &= - \\dfrac{4}{\\cancel{3} } \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\\\[5pt]\nx &= -4.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054420 1074 "First, we isolate the fractional term $\\dfrac{3x}{4}$ by adding $5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5 &=-8\\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}-5+5 &=-8+5\\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4}+0 &=-3 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{3x}{4} &=-3\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ so we multiply by its reciprocal $\\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x &=-3 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{3}{4}x &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\cdot (-3) \\\\[5pt]\n1 x &= - \\dfrac{4}{\\cancel{3} } \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\\\[5pt]\nx &= -4.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054480 1071 "What is the fourth term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n) \\,, \\quad f(1)=-1\\,, \\quad f(2) = 3, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054480 1071 "What is the fourth term of the sequence\n\n$f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n) \\,, \\quad f(1)=-1\\,, \\quad f(2) = 3, \\quad n \\geq 1$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054512 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054512 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054513 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054513 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054513 1074 "From the graph, we find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.\n$$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{\\left(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)}^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-12}{8}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-3}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054513 1074 "From the graph, we find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{\\left(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)}^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-12}{8}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-3}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054514 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054514 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054516 1071 "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $$\n\\displaystyle P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)\n$$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054516 1071 "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054517 1071 "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $$\n\\displaystyle P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)\n$$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054517 1071 "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054518 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054518 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054519 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054519 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054520 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054520 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054521 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054521 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054521 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054521 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054568 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\nP(A) = \\dfrac{n}{N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to pick $5$ tiles out of $12$, and $n$ is the number of those $5$-tile picks where $2$ tiles have the letter $b$ and $3$ tiles have the letter $c$.\n\nNote that since the order of the picks does not matter, we must use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N$. The number of ways to draw $5$ tiles from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\nN = {}_{12}C_5 = \\dfrac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!} = 792\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n$. The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps:\n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $4$) tiles with the letter $b$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {}_4 C_2 = \\dfrac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = 6\n$$\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $4$) tiles with the letter $c$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {}_4 C_3 = \\dfrac{4!}{3! \\cdot 1!} = 4\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\nn = {}_4 C_2 \\cdot {}_4 C_3 = 6 \\cdot 4 = 24\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nP(A) = \\dfrac{24}{792} \\approx 0.03=3\\%\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054568 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\nP(A) = \\dfrac{n}{N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to pick $5$ tiles out of $12$, and $n$ is the number of those $5$-tile picks where $2$ tiles have the letter $b$ and $3$ tiles have the letter $c$.\n\nNote that since the order of the picks does not matter, we must use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N$. The number of ways to draw $5$ tiles from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\nN = {}_{12}C_5 = \\dfrac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!} = 792\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n$. The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps:\n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $4$) tiles with the letter $b$ is\n${}_4 C_2 = \\dfrac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = 6$\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $4$) tiles with the letter $c$ is\n${}_4 C_3 = \\dfrac{4!}{3! \\cdot 1!} = 4$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\nn = {}_4 C_2 \\cdot {}_4 C_3 = 6 \\cdot 4 = 24\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nP(A) = \\dfrac{24}{792} \\approx 0.03=3\\%\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186054580 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{2\\sin{x}\\cos{2x}}=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186054580 1071 "$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\c