urth term of the following sequence?\n\n$f(n + 2) = f(n + 1) + f(n), f(1) = 1, f(2) = 4, n \\ge 1$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055476 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055476 1071 "Calculate $\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055476 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-2y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055476 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-2y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055938 1064 "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us about the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are *two distinct real solutions.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is *one distinct real solution.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are *no real solutions.*\n\nIf we are given a quadratic equation with an unknown parameter, then we can use the criterion $D > 0$ to determine the values of the unknown parameter such that the quadratic equation has two *distinct* real solutions.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+kx+9=0\n$$\n\nLet's find all the values of $k$ such that this equation has two distinct real solutions.\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$. To compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients of the equation are\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=k,\\quad c=9\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & > 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & > 0 \\\\ (k)^{2} - 4(1)(9) & > 0 \\\\ k^{2} - 36 & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}> 36 \\\\ \\sqrt{k^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}> \\sqrt{36}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid > 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nk > 6\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad k < -6\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write our solution as\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,-6) \\cup (6,\\infty)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ inside this interval will give a quadratic equation with *distinct* real solutions." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055938 1064 "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us about the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are *two distinct real solutions.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is *one distinct real solution.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are *no real solutions.*\n\nIf we are given a quadratic equation with an unknown parameter, then we can use the criterion $D > 0$ to determine the values of the unknown parameter such that the quadratic equation has two *distinct* real solutions.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+kx+9=0\n$$\n\nLet's find all the values of $k$ such that this equation has two distinct real solutions.\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$. To compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients of the equation are\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=k,\\quad c=9\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & > 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac & > 0 \\\\ (k)^{2} - 4(1)(9) & > 0 \\\\ k^{2} - 36 & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n$k^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}> 36 \\\\ \\sqrt{k^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}> \\sqrt{36}; \\mid k \\mid \\mid > 6$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nk > 6\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad k < -6\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write our solution as\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,-6) \\cup (6,\\infty)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ inside this interval will give a quadratic equation with *distinct* real solutions." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055954 1064 "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055954 1064 "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055955 1064 "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055955 1064 "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055956 1064 "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055956 1064 "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055964 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Subtraction**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3\n$$\n- **Multiplication**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2\n$$\n- **Division**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055964 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186055989 1064 "Functions are usually given in the form $y=f(x)$ where $y$ is explicitly written as a function of $x.$ However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n$$\n2x^3+y^2=3.\n$$\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x.$\n\nWe need to remember that $y$ *is a function of* $x,$ i.e., $y = y(x),$ So, we apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 3 ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y} ( {y}^{2} ) ( \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} ) &  = 0 \\\\ 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - 6 {x}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{y}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the derivative of $y$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=- \\dfrac{3x^2}{y}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186055989 1064 "Functions are usually given in the form $y=f(x)$ where $y$ is explicitly written as a function of $x.$ However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n$$\n2x^3+y^2=3.\n$$\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x.$\n\nWe need to remember that $y$ *is a function of* $x,$ i.e., $y = y(x),$ So, we apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 3 ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y} ( {y}^{2} ) ( \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} ) &  = 0 \\\\ 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - 6 {x}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{y}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the derivative of $y$ is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=- \\dfrac{3x^2}{y}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186056031 1064 "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.\n$$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\\\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\\\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186056031 1064 "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\\\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\\\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186056069 1064 "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186056069 1064 "Let's think about how to factor the following 