, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186063916 1074 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III