1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$”; “The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$” only.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064612 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$”; “The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$” only.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186064644 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}dy\n$$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064644 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}}dy$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186064645 1071 "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{3}{x^{2} + 1}dx\n$$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064645 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{3}{x^{2} + 1}dx$?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186064646 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064646 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186064647 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064647 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186064656 1071 "Which number line shows the solution of the following system of inequalities?\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 k + 18 \\le  - 3 \\\\ 4 k - 6 \\ge  2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186064656 1071 "Which number line shows the solution of the fo